<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-column-vectors-in-terms-of-unit-vectors" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Column Vectors in Terms of Unit Vectors</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.6 Vectors II
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Express column and position vectors in terms of unit vectors in different situations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Expressing vectors in terms of unit vectors i, j and k.
         Suggested activity: Learners express given column vectors in the form xi + yj + zk and back. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Core 2.6 (a); (b) add, subtract, scalar multiply in 3D placed here (no section claims it)
         Activity: as planning notes (columns to i, j, k and back) | added: combining vectors, parts (e), (f)
         JSXGraph: none named in the planning notes
         STACK: assets/stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/12-vectors-ii/03-column-vectors-in-terms-of-unit-vectors/ -->

    <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
        <ol>
            <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> the unit vectors <m>\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>\mathbf{j}</m>, <m>\mathbf{k}</m>; a column <m>\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix}</m> is <m>x\mathbf{i} + y\mathbf{j} + z\mathbf{k}</m>; vectors are added, subtracted and multiplied by scalars component by component.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> change a vector from one form to the other; add, subtract and multiply vectors by scalars in either form; solve simple vector equations.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> combine two displacements of a drone into one.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
            <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners see that <m>\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>\mathbf{j}</m> and <m>\mathbf{k}</m> are building blocks for every vector, and that operations work component by component.</p></li>
            <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Work in pairs: one learner writes each answer as a column, the other in <m>\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>\mathbf{j}</m>, <m>\mathbf{k}</m> form. Swap roles at part (d). Ask after part (d): <q>Where did the <m>\mathbf{j}</m> go?</q></p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-columns-to-ijk">
        <title>From Columns to <m>\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>\mathbf{j}</m>, <m>\mathbf{k}</m> and Back</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Let <m>\mathbf{i} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\mathbf{j} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\mathbf{k} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Draw <m>\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>\mathbf{j}</m> and <m>\mathbf{k}</m> on a sketch of the three axes.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Each is an arrow of length <m>1</m> from <m>O</m>: <m>\mathbf{i}</m> along <m>Ox</m>, <m>\mathbf{j}</m> along <m>Oy</m>, <m>\mathbf{k}</m> along <m>Oz</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Write <m>\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -3 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}</m> as multiples of <m>\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>\mathbf{j}</m> or <m>\mathbf{k}</m>.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>2\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>-3\mathbf{j}</m>, <m>4\mathbf{k}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Add the three columns in (b). Write the result as a column and in terms of <m>\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>\mathbf{j}</m> and <m>\mathbf{k}</m>.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} = 2\mathbf{i} - 3\mathbf{j} + 4\mathbf{k}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Write <m>4\mathbf{i} - \mathbf{k}</m> as a column.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 0 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}</m>. There is no <m>\mathbf{j}</m> term, so the middle component is <m>0</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><m>\mathbf{a} = 2\mathbf{i} + \mathbf{j} - 3\mathbf{k}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b} = \mathbf{i} - 4\mathbf{j} + 2\mathbf{k}</m>. Find <m>\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}</m> in both forms.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>3\mathbf{i} - 3\mathbf{j} - \mathbf{k} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Find <m>3\mathbf{a} - 2\mathbf{b}</m>.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>3\mathbf{a} = 6\mathbf{i} + 3\mathbf{j} - 9\mathbf{k}</m> and <m>2\mathbf{b} = 2\mathbf{i} - 8\mathbf{j} + 4\mathbf{k}</m>, so <m>3\mathbf{a} - 2\mathbf{b} = 4\mathbf{i} + 11\mathbf{j} - 13\mathbf{k}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise the unit vectors <m>\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>\mathbf{j}</m>, <m>\mathbf{k}</m> and the operations on vectors in three dimensions.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> parts (c) and (d) wrote the same vector in two ways.</p></li>
                <li><p>A zero component is left out of <m>\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>\mathbf{j}</m>, <m>\mathbf{k}</m> form but kept as <m>0</m> in a column.</p></li>
                <li><p>Parts (e) and (f): add or subtract matching components; a scalar multiplies every component.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight xml:id="insight-unit-vectors">
        <title>Unit Vectors <m>\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>\mathbf{j}</m> and <m>\mathbf{k}</m></title>
        <p>
            A <term>unit vector</term> is a vector of magnitude <m>1</m>. The unit vectors along the axes <m>Ox</m>, <m>Oy</m> and <m>Oz</m> are
            <md>\mathbf{i} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}, \quad \mathbf{j} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}, \quad \mathbf{k} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}</md>
            Each is one step along an axis. Every vector can be built from them:
            <md>\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix} = x\mathbf{i} + y\mathbf{j} + z\mathbf{k}</md>
            A zero component is left out (<m>4\mathbf{i} - \mathbf{k}</m>), and a coefficient <m>1</m> is not written (<m>\mathbf{i}</m>, not <m>1\mathbf{i}</m>). The point <m>P(x, y, z)</m> has position vector <m>x\mathbf{i} + y\mathbf{j} + z\mathbf{k}</m>.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <insight xml:id="insight-operations-in-three-dimensions">
        <title>Operations in Three Dimensions</title>
        <p>
            To add or subtract vectors, add or subtract matching components. To multiply a vector by a number <m>k</m> (a <term>scalar</term>), multiply every component by <m>k</m>:
            <md>\begin{pmatrix} a_1 \\ a_2 \\ a_3 \end{pmatrix} \pm \begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \\ b_3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} a_1 \pm b_1 \\ a_2 \pm b_2 \\ a_3 \pm b_3 \end{pmatrix}, \qquad k\begin{pmatrix} a_1 \\ a_2 \\ a_3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} ka_1 \\ ka_2 \\ ka_3 \end{pmatrix}</md>
            In <m>\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>\mathbf{j}</m>, <m>\mathbf{k}</m> form, collect the <m>\mathbf{i}</m> terms, the <m>\mathbf{j}</m> terms and the <m>\mathbf{k}</m> terms. A scalar multiplies every component; it never adds to them.
        </p>
        <p>
            The <term>zero vector</term> <m>\mathbf{0}</m> has every component <m>0</m>; <m>\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{a} = \mathbf{0}</m>.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <figure xml:id="fig-unit-vectors">
        <caption>The unit vectors <m>\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>\mathbf{j}</m>, <m>\mathbf{k}</m>, and <m>2\mathbf{i} - 3\mathbf{j} + 4\mathbf{k}</m> built from <m>2\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>-3\mathbf{j}</m> and <m>4\mathbf{k}</m>.</caption>
        <image width="55%">
            <shortdescription>Short thick arrows i, j and k along the x-, y- and z-axes. Arrows 2i, then minus 3j, then 4k are placed tip to tail and reach the point (2, minus 3, 4); a dashed arrow goes straight from O to that point.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
\tdplotsetmaincoords{70}{110}
\begin{tikzpicture}[tdplot_main_coords, scale=0.9, >=Stealth]
\draw[->] (0,0,0) -- (5,0,0) node[anchor=north east]{$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-4.5,0) -- (0,3,0) node[anchor=west]{$y$};
\draw[->] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,6.2) node[anchor=south]{$z$};
\node[anchor=north west] at (0,0,0) {$O$};
\draw[->, ultra thick] (0,0,0) -- (1,0,0);
\node[anchor=south east] at (1,0,0) {$\mathbf{i}$};
\draw[->, ultra thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,1,0);
\node[anchor=south] at (0,1,0) {$\mathbf{j}$};
\draw[->, ultra thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,1);
\node[anchor=west] at (0,0,1) {$\mathbf{k}$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (2,0,0);
\node[anchor=north west] at (1.5,0,0) {$2\mathbf{i}$};
\draw[->, thick] (2,0,0) -- (2,-3,0);
\node[anchor=north] at (2,-1.5,0) {$-3\mathbf{j}$};
\draw[->, thick] (2,-3,0) -- (2,-3,4);
\node[anchor=east] at (2,-3,2) {$4\mathbf{k}$};
\draw[->, thick, densely dashed] (0,0,0) -- (2,-3,4);
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.3pt, label={above:{$(2, -3, 4)$}}] at (2,-3,4) {};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <note xml:id="note-vectors-in-a-spreadsheet">
        <title>Vectors in a Spreadsheet</title>
        <p>
            A spreadsheet does vector arithmetic one column at a time. Put the headings <c>x</c>, <c>y</c>, <c>z</c> in cells <c>B1</c> to <c>D1</c>, the components of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> in row 2 and those of <m>\mathbf{b}</m> in row 3. In <c>B4</c> type <c>=2*B2-3*B3</c> and fill it right to <c>D4</c>: row 4 is <m>2\mathbf{a} - 3\mathbf{b}</m>. In <c>E2</c>, <c>=SQRT(SUMSQ(B2:D2))</c> gives the magnitude of <m>\mathbf{a}</m>, which you meet in <xref ref="sec-magnitude-of-a-vector-in-three-dimensions"/>. Change a component and every result updates.
        </p>
    </note>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-changing-the-form">
        <title>Changing the Form</title>
        <statement>
            <p>(a) Write <m>\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 7 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</m> in terms of <m>\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>\mathbf{j}</m> and <m>\mathbf{k}</m>. (b) Write <m>5\mathbf{i} - 3\mathbf{k}</m> as a column. (c) Write the position vector of <m>P(4, -1, 6)</m> in terms of <m>\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>\mathbf{j}</m> and <m>\mathbf{k}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>(a) <m>-2\mathbf{i} + 7\mathbf{j}</m>: the <m>\mathbf{k}</m> component is <m>0</m>, so it is left out.</p>
            <p>(b) There is no <m>\mathbf{j}</m> term, so the middle component is <m>0</m>: <m>\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 0 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
            <p>(c) <m>\overrightarrow{OP} = 4\mathbf{i} - \mathbf{j} + 6\mathbf{k}</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-adding-and-subtracting">
        <title>Adding and Subtracting in Three Dimensions</title>
        <statement>
            <p><m>\mathbf{p} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\mathbf{q} = \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}</m>. Find <m>\mathbf{p} + \mathbf{q}</m>, <m>\mathbf{p} - \mathbf{q}</m> and <m>2\mathbf{p} + 3\mathbf{q}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p><m>\mathbf{p} + \mathbf{q} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 + (-2) \\ -1 + 5 \\ 4 + 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\mathbf{p} - \mathbf{q} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 - (-2) \\ -1 - 5 \\ 4 - 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ -6 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
            <p>Multiply first, then add: <md>2\mathbf{p} + 3\mathbf{q} = \begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -2 \\ 8 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} -6 \\ 15 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 13 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}</md></p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-unknown-constants">
        <title>Unknown Constants</title>
        <statement>
            <p><m>\mathbf{a} = p\mathbf{i} + 2\mathbf{j} - \mathbf{k}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b} = 3\mathbf{i} + q\mathbf{j} + r\mathbf{k}</m>, and <m>\mathbf{a} + 2\mathbf{b} = 5\mathbf{i} - 4\mathbf{j} + 7\mathbf{k}</m>. Find <m>p</m>, <m>q</m> and <m>r</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p><m>\mathbf{a} + 2\mathbf{b} = (p + 6)\mathbf{i} + (2 + 2q)\mathbf{j} + (-1 + 2r)\mathbf{k}</m>. Equal vectors have equal components, so
            <md>
                <mrow>p + 6 \amp = 5, \amp p \amp = -1</mrow>
                <mrow>2 + 2q \amp = -4, \amp q \amp = -3</mrow>
                <mrow>-1 + 2r \amp = 7, \amp r \amp = 4</mrow>
            </md></p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

    <p>Type one number in each box of a column vector. Fractions may be typed with a slash, for example <c>5/2</c>. Type vectors in <m>\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>\mathbf{j}</m>, <m>\mathbf{k}</m> form like <c>3i-2j+5k</c>; leave out a zero component.</p>

